        "yoast_head": "<!-- This site is optimized with the Yoast SEO plugin v28.5 - https://yoast.com/product/yoast-seo-wordpress/ -->\n<title>Further Thoughts on the Origins of the Term &quot;Biblicism&quot; - Alpha and Omega Ministries</title>\n<meta name=\"robots\" content=\"index, follow, max-snippet:-1, max-image-preview:large, max-video-preview:-1\" />\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://www.aomin.org/aoblog/church-history/further-thoughts-on-the-origins-of-the-term-biblicism/\" />\n<meta property=\"og:locale\" content=\"en_US\" />\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\" />\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"Further Thoughts on the Origins of the Term &quot;Biblicism&quot; - Alpha and Omega Ministries\" />\n<meta property=\"og:description\" content=\"As there continues to be pushback against those of us who would refer to ourselves as &#8220;Biblicists&#8221; (or, better yet, as &#8220;Reformed Biblicists&#8220;), I have been looking at the history of the usage of the term. This is not very hard to do, yet some professors and pastors seem to have difficulty with it. In my previous article, which detailed [&hellip;]\" />\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://www.aomin.org/aoblog/church-history/further-thoughts-on-the-origins-of-the-term-biblicism/\" />\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"Alpha and Omega Ministries\" />\n<meta property=\"article:publisher\" content=\"https://www.facebook.com/aomin.org\" />\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2024-06-17T19:07:08+00:00\" />\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2024-06-17T19:07:11+00:00\" />\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://www.aomin.org/aoblog/wp-content/uploads/2020/06/Logo-red-e1591639042841.png\" />\n\t<meta property=\"og:image:width\" content=\"257\" />\n\t<meta property=\"og:image:height\" content=\"234\" />\n\t<meta property=\"og:image:type\" content=\"image/png\" />\n<meta name=\"author\" content=\"Chris Whisonant\" />\n<meta name=