      "sourceMetadata": {
        "id": 9631,
        "date": "2022-03-28T10:18:41",
        "date_gmt": "2022-03-28T14:18:41",
        "guid": {
          "rendered": "http://michaeljkruger.com/?p=9631"
        },
        "modified": "2023-04-14T18:03:25",
        "modified_gmt": "2023-04-14T22:03:25",
        "slug": "looking-for-brief-and-accessible-books-on-the-origins-of-the-biblical-canon-here-are-two-good-ones",
        "status": "publish",
        "type": "post",
        "link": "https://michaeljkruger.com/looking-for-brief-and-accessible-books-on-the-origins-of-the-biblical-canon-here-are-two-good-ones/",
        "title": {
          "rendered": "Looking for Brief and Accessible Books on the Origins of the Biblical Canon? Here are Two Good Ones"
        },
        "content": {
          "rendered": "<p>Whenever I speak on the origins of the New Testament canon, I am regularly asked about whether there are brief, accessible books on the subject—the kind that could be given to lay folks in the church. Unfortunately, my books on canon usually don&#8217;t qualify (e.g., <a href=\"https://amzn.to/3NupnXC\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><em>Canon Revisited</em></a> clocks in at over 300 pages).</p>\n<p>For years, I have been asked to write a shorter version, but just haven&#8217;t had the time. Thankfully, others have stepped in to fill that gap. Let me mention two wonderful little books that have just come out in the last few years.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://amzn.to/3qIMG6f\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\"n3VNCb alignright\" src=\"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/41deHzexSKL._AC_SY780_.jpg\" alt=\"Who Chose the Books of the New Testament? (Questions for Restless Minds): Hill, Charles E., Carson, D. A.: 9781683595199: Amazon.com: Books\" width=\"250\" height=\"350\" data-noaft=\"1\" /></a>Just this year, Chuck Hill, professor emeritus of New Testament at RTS Orlando, has released, <a href=\"https://amzn.to/3qIMG6f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><em>Who Chose the Books of the New Testament?</em> </a>(Lexham, 2022).  This little volume is part of the <em>Questions for Restless Minds</em> series edited by D.A. Carson.</p>\n<p>In 81 short pages, this book covers a lot of ground. It deals with the Walter Bauer-inspired idea that the canon is merely the result of politics and power, as well as the popular evangelical approach which argues that we can solve the canon problem merely by adopting so-called &#8220;criteria of canonicity.&#8221;</p>\n<p>Instead of either of these approaches, this volume advocates a self-authenticating model of canon where these books, in some sense, imposed themselves on the church because of their distinctive qualities. Then, Chuck shows how the historical data supports this idea that the books were received naturally and early within the burgeoning Christian movement.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://amzn.to/3qE8T5G\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\"n3VNCb alignright\" src=\"https://g.christianbook.com/g/slideshow/7/7102682/main/7102682_1_ftc.jpg\" alt=\"A Christian's Pocket Guide to How We Got the Bible: Gregory R. Lanier: 9781527102682 - Christianbook.com\" width=\"210\" height=\"345\" data-noaft=\"1\" /></a>The other volume is <a href=\"https://amzn.to/3qE8T5G\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><em>A Christian&#8217;s Pocket Guide to How We Got the Bible</em> </a>(Christian Focus, 2018), by Greg Lanier, Associate Professor of New Testament at RTS Orlando.</p>\n<p>In just 107 pages, this little volume also packs a punch. It approaches the origins of the canon in a very similar way as Chuck Hill above, but this volume offers two distinctives.  For one, it also covers the OT canon—a subject which is quite complex and always needs attention.</p>\n<p>And second, it also covers the issue of textual transmission. In other words, Lanier asks not only whether we have the right books, but also whether we have the right <em>words</em>. For those looking for a brief introduction to the issue of textual reliability, this is a good place to start.</p>\n<p>Both of these little volumes are excellent pathways into the world of the biblical canon. If you are looking for something short and to the point—for yourself or for a friend—then you will want to get a hold of each of them.</p>\n",
          "protected": false
        },
        "excerpt": {
          "rendered": "<p>Whenever I speak on the origins of the New Testament canon, I am regularly asked about whether there are brief, accessible books on the subject—the kind that could be given to lay folks in the church. Unfortunately, my books on canon usually don&#8217;t qualify (e.g., Canon Revisited clocks in at over 300 pages). For years, [&hellip;]</p>\n",
          "protected": false
        },
