    {
      "source": "kruger",
      "sourceId": "8688",
      "title": "Think You Know the Christmas Story? Here are Five Common Misconceptions",
      "author": "Michael Kruger",
      "url": "https://michaeljkruger.com/think-you-know-the-christmas-story-here-are-five-common-misconceptions-2/",
      "sha256": "96d0f7e45f76747a667665787a378bbca4a56eb21ef61df90fb9d4c5b3af791f",
      "bytes": 7622,
      "format": "html",
      "path": "D:\\FortressOfSolitude\\Jarvis\\Projects\\BibleProject\\sources\\library\\bulk\\kruger\\8688.html",
      "acquiredAt": "2026-10-07T20:13:51.455999+00:00",
      "language": "en",
      "audience": "adult readers; pastors; scholars",
      "campaignMission": "RB08",
      "licence": "Copyright retained by author/ministry; publicly offered written material; private local research copy; original notices and credits retained",
      "useScope": "private local import and embedding",
      "contributor": "Michael Kruger",
      "credit": "kruger",
      "sourceApi": "https://michaeljkruger.com/wp-json/wp/v2/posts?per_page=100&page=4",
      "sourceMetadata": {
        "id": 8688,
        "date": "2020-12-14T13:45:12",
        "date_gmt": "2020-12-14T18:45:12",
        "guid": {
          "rendered": "http://michaeljkruger.com/?p=8688"
        },
        "modified": "2023-04-14T18:00:50",
        "modified_gmt": "2023-04-14T22:00:50",
        "slug": "think-you-know-the-christmas-story-here-are-five-common-misconceptions-2",
        "status": "publish",
        "type": "post",
        "link": "https://michaeljkruger.com/think-you-know-the-christmas-story-here-are-five-common-misconceptions-2/",
        "title": {
          "rendered": "Think You Know the Christmas Story? Here are Five Common Misconceptions"
        },
        "content": {
          "rendered": "<p>Bah, humbug.</p>\n<p>That&#8217;s probably one of the most well-known lines in literary (and now, cinematic) history. Everybody immediately recognizes the curmudgeonly voice of Ebeneezer Scrooge as he pours cold water all over our Christmas spirit.</p>\n<p>And his point is still made today by some, albeit in different words.  It&#8217;s that the Christmas story just isn&#8217;t true. It&#8217;s rubbish. It&#8217;s made up. It&#8217;s all in our heads.</p>\n<p>While now is not the time for a full-scale defense of the historicity of the Christmas story, Scrooge&#8217;s skepticism does prompt us to wonder whether we&#8217;ve gotten the story right. Are we telling the story that really was, or are we just telling the story that we want to tell?  Are we just telling the story in our heads?</p>\n<p>It might be useful, then, to reflect on a few common misconceptions about the Christmas story. The best way to validate the Christmas story to the Ebeneezer Scrooge&#8217;s out there is to make sure we&#8217;ve got it right ourselves. Here are five of them.<!--more--></p>\n<h4>1. There Was a Star the Night Jesus Was Born</h4>\n<p>It’s difficult to find a nativity scene (or Christmas play) without a star over the manger. Indeed, this might be the quintessential symbol of the birth of Jesus.</p>\n<p>The problem is there’s no indication the star hovered over the manger on the night Jesus was born. On the contrary, when the angels announced the birth of Jesus to the shepherds watching their flocks by night (<a class=\"esv-crossref-link\" href=\"https://www.esv.org/Luke%202%3A8%E2%80%9311/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Luke 2:8–11</a>), they weren’t told to look for a star. They were told to look for something else: “And this will be a sign for you: you will find a baby wrapped in swaddling cloths and lying in a manger” (<a class=\"esv-crossref-link\" href=\"https://www.esv.org/Luke%202%3A12/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Luke 2:12</a>).</p>\n<p>The star was given not to the shepherds but to <a href=\"http://www.thegospelcoalition.org/article/three-kings-orient-arent/\">the Magi</a> (<a class=\"esv-crossref-link\" href=\"https://www.esv.org/Matt.%202%3A2/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Matt. 2:2</a>), who appear to be visiting Jesus at a later time period. How much later is unclear, but the fact that Herod commands all the babies in the region younger than <em>2 years old</em> to be killed suggests Jesus may have been in Bethlehem for some time.</p>\n<h4>2. There Were Three Wise Men</h4>\n<p>Speaking of the wise men, in both art and in song (“We Three Kings”) we get the undeniable impression there were <em>three</em> of them (also called “Magi”). The problem, however, is that this number is <a href=\"http://www.thegospelcoalition.org/article/three-kings-orient-arent/\">found nowhere</a> in the biblical accounts.</p>\n<p>Matthew simply tells us, “Now after Jesus was born in Bethlehem of Judea in the days of Herod the king, behold, wise men from the east came to Jerusalem” (<a class=\"esv-crossref-link\" href=\"https://www.esv.org/Matt.%202%3A1/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Matt. 2:1</a>).</p>\n<p>The idea of three wise men likely came from the fact that Matthew mentions three gifts: gold, frankincense, and myrrh (<a class=\"esv-crossref-link\" href=\"https://www.esv.org/Matt.%202%3A11/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Matt. 2:11</a>).</p>\n<h4>3. There Was No Room for Them in the Inn</h4>\n<p>Another staple of modern nativity plays is the scene at the inn. Joseph and his wife, Mary—who is on the verge of giving birth—are cruelly turned away by the innkeeper who shows them no compassion.</p>\n<p>Here is another popular misconception. The Bible never mentions an innkeeper. In fact, it’s possible there was never even an “inn” at all.</p>\n<p>A recent study by Stephen Carlson <a href=\"http://www.hypotyposeis.org/papers/Carlson%202010%20NTS.pdf\">makes the case</a> (a compelling one, I think) that the Greek word normally translated as “inn” (καταλύματι) in <a class=\"esv-crossref-link\" href=\"https://www.esv.org/Luke%202%3A7/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Luke 2:7</a> is best translated as “place to stay.” So the passage isn’t saying there was no room in the inn, but rather there was no room for baby Jesus in the <em>place they stayed</em>. In what place were they staying? Carlson argues it was probably in the home of Joseph’s family in Bethlehem, perhaps in an adjacent guest room (which would have been small).</p>\n<h4>4. Jesus Was Born in a Barn or Stable</h4>\n<p>Since Joseph could find no spot in the inn, the reasoning goes, he must have been forced to stay in the stable. Indeed, every nativity scene ever created places Jesus in a barn of sorts.</p>\n<p>But the text doesn’t say he was born in a barn. It only says Mary “laid in him in a manger” (<a class=\"esv-crossref-link\" href=\"https://www.esv.org/Luke%202%3A7/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Luke 2:7</a>). Although that might seem to suggest a barn, it was common for mangers to be kept in the main room of village houses during this time period. Why? Because the animals were often housed just a few feet away in an adjacent room.</p>\n<p>It seems likely, then, that Mary gave birth to Jesus while they were staying at the home of Joseph’s relatives in Bethlehem. But the room in which they stayed—likely a tight guest room or hastily added chamber—couldn’t accommodate a birth. So, Mary had to give birth in the larger family room and lay Jesus in the nearby manger.</p>\n<h4>5. Jesus Was Born on December 25</h4>\n<p>If we celebrate Jesus’s birthday on December 25, it’s logical to think this was the day on which he was born. But the precise date of Jesus’s birth is uncertain. A variety of options have been suggested throughout church history, including March 21, April 15, and May 20.</p>\n<p>So how did December 25 become the date? It’s commonly thought Christians took over the pagan holiday of <em>Sol Invictus</em> (“Unconquered Sun”), which was on December 25. But there’s little evidence to back this up.</p>\n<p>Instead it seems early Christians may have reasoned from the supposed date of Mary’s conception, which was thought to be March 25—the same day Jesus was thought to be crucified. Fast forward nine months and you land on December 25 for his birth.</p>\n<p>These five misconceptions remind us that sometimes our picture of scriptural stories is shaped more by popular perceptions and modern retellings than by the text itself. But when we take a closer look at the biblical clues, a wonderful—and hopefully more accurate—picture emerges of what happened that night nearly 2,000 years ago.</p>\n<p>And what happened that night still stands as one of the most monumental events in human history. God became a man and entered our dark, cold world to redeem a sinful people.</p>\n<p>And that is a story that should transform our &#8220;Bah, humbug&#8221; into a hearty &#8220;Merry Christmas.&#8221;</p>\n<p><em>Note: A version of this article originally appeared on <a href=\"https://www.thegospelcoalition.org/article/5-popular-misconceptions-christmas-story/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">The Gospel Coalition</a> website.</em></p>\n",
          "protected": false
        },
        "excerpt": {
          "rendered": "<p>Bah, humbug. That&#8217;s probably one of the most well-known lines in literary (and now, cinematic) history. Everybody immediately recognizes the curmudgeonly voice of Ebeneezer Scrooge as he pours cold water all over our Christmas spirit. And his point is still made today by some, albeit in different words.  It&#8217;s that the Christmas story just isn&#8217;t [&hellip;]</p>\n",
          "protected": false
        },
