        "modified": "2023-04-14T17:54:49",
        "modified_gmt": "2023-04-14T21:54:49",
        "slug": "did-jesus-promise-to-return-within-the-lifetime-of-his-disciples",
        "status": "publish",
        "type": "post",
        "link": "https://michaeljkruger.com/did-jesus-promise-to-return-within-the-lifetime-of-his-disciples/",
        "title": {
          "rendered": "Did Jesus Promise to Return within the Lifetime of his Disciples?"
        },
        "content": {
          "rendered": "<p>For critics of early Christianity, one of the stock objections is that Jesus predicted he would return in the lifetime of his disciples.  And since he clearly didn&#8217;t return, then he cannot be believed.</p>\n<p>Indeed, it is this belief that has spurred many other theories among scholars, most notably Hans Conzelmann&#8217;s thesis that Luke&#8217;s Gospel was written to explain the delay of the second coming.</p>\n<p>But, is it really true that Jesus predicted that he would return in the lifetime of his disciples?  Here&#8217;s a recent video where I briefly address this question:</p>\n<p><iframe title=\"#Wisdom Wednesday with Dr. Michael Kruger “Did Jesus say He would return in the 1st century A.D.?”\" src=\"https://player.vimeo.com/video/258120281?dnt=1&amp;app_id=122963\" width=\"800\" height=\"450\" frameborder=\"0\" allow=\"autoplay; fullscreen; picture-in-picture; clipboard-write\"></iframe></p>\n<p>As for whether the imminent coming of Jesus affected the development of the NT canon, see my other article <a href=\"https://michaeljkruger.com/did-early-christians-believe-that-jesus-would-return-in-their-own-lifetime-implications-for-canon/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">here</a>.</p>\n",
          "protected": false
        },
        "excerpt": {
          "rendered": "<p>For critics of early Christianity, one of the stock objections is that Jesus predicted he would return in the lifetime of his disciples.  And since he clearly didn&#8217;t return, then he cannot be believed. Indeed, it is this belief that has spurred many other theories among scholars, most notably Hans Conzelmann&#8217;s thesis that Luke&#8217;s Gospel [&hellip;]</p>\n",
          "protected": false
        },
